          },
          {
            "op": "journal",
            "text": "The clerk behind the granary went in the pit as a suicide. Nobody will ask again."
          }
        ],
        "lore": []
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "porters-break",
      "type": "story",
      "position": {
        "x": 1000,
        "y": 420
      },
      "data": {
        "kind": "beat",
        "title": "The porters' noon break",
        "description": "With the cart in the yard, the granary porters come in from the far gate for their noon break, bread and cheese in their fists, to see the clerk cut down decent. Old Wat, the oldest of them, comes straight to the Sarge, cap in hand, and tells her unasked what he saw at first light: Big Hobb, the Drowned Rat's arm-wrestling champion, coming out of this yard's gate with a gentleman in a good coat, his right hand wrapped in a rag. 'Hobb does night work for gents.' It's the lead to the Drowned Rat, given whole: who, by name, and where to find him. The other porters stay by the gate with their bread, watching, and say nothing in front of the watch. Tull waits on, a step nearer going every exchange.",
        "cast": {
          "wat": "Cap off to the dead clerk, then straight to the Sarge, unasked: 'Sarge. A word. About first light.' Tells her plainly what he saw (Big Hobb, the Rat's champion, out of this gate with a gentleman in a good coat, his right hand wrapped), Hobb by name and where he drinks; then answers the investigator's questions, no further than he saw.",
          "kell": "Hears Wat out and writes it down, reading it back in the words of the record: 'Big Hobb Mallory, the Drowned Rat. Noted.' Says nothing of what it means: it's the investigator's case. With the lead in her notebook now, she signs Prout in as a murder once the case holds.",
          "tull": "Waits on the Sarge's word, and nods to the porters, whom he knows; each exchange he waits he does one more thing towards going, never the same thing twice.",
          "cob": "Goes very still at the name Hobb; his face answers if anyone looks at him.",
          "moll": "Watches the gate and the porters, some of whom know her. Fierce if anyone says 'the Mending House'."
        },
        "pressure": "The porters' break doesn't last: they eat, take a last look at the clerk and drift back to the far gate. Tull's patience goes on running down from where it stood, a step every exchange and never the same step twice, to the Sarge asked, for the last time, what he's to write.",
        "lore": []
      }
    }
  ],
  "edges": [
    {
      "id": "find-murder",
      "source": "start",
      "target": "murder",
      "type": "smoothstep",
